From: Teal Bauer <teal@starsong.eu>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 23:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netlink: do not read completion_fn out of a freed transaction

transaction_complete() removes the transaction from self->transactions before
calling tr->completion_fn(). That hash table is created with transaction_free()
as its GDestroyNotify, and transaction_free() ends in g_slice_free(), so the
remove frees tr. The next statement reads tr->completion_fn out of the freed
block and calls it.

The same function already caches tr->sequence_id into a local precisely because
tr is gone by the time the error branch formats its message. The success branch
was missed. Cache completion_fn the same way.

Observed on an armv7 board running 1.24.2: SIGSEGV with pc=0x00000018 about two
seconds into "simple connect", repeating until the connection happened to
succeed. The core showed the Transaction slab already recycled, with a glibc
tcache next pointer at offset 0, the tcache key at offset 4, and 0x18 sitting
where completion_fn belongs.

Introduced in 0a94b0138 ("netlink: add RTM_GETLINK support to read MAC
address"), which added the completion_fn indirection. Present in 1.24.0, 1.24.2
and main.

transaction_complete_with_error() does not need the same treatment: it touches
nothing in tr after the remove.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Teal Bauer <teal@starsong.eu>
---
--- a/src/mm-netlink.c
+++ b/src/mm-netlink.c
@@ -229,9 +229,11 @@
 {
     GTask *task;
     guint32 sequence_id;
+    MsgFunc completion_fn;
 
     task = g_steal_pointer (&tr->completion_task);
     sequence_id = tr->sequence_id;
+    completion_fn = tr->completion_fn;
 
     g_hash_table_remove (tr->self->transactions,
                          GUINT_TO_POINTER (tr->sequence_id));
@@ -239,7 +241,7 @@
     if (!saved_errno) {
         GError *error = NULL;
 
-        if (!tr->completion_fn (task, hdr, &error))
+        if (!completion_fn (task, hdr, &error))
             g_task_return_error (task, error);
     } else {
         g_task_return_new_error (task, G_IO_ERROR, g_io_error_from_errno (saved_errno),
-- 

